## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over multiple segments with a budget and in/out state tracking to allow adjacency.

## Key Concepts
- DP with segment count and in/out state
- Transitions for starting, extending, and ending a segment
- Handling adjacency and at-most-k constraint

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a, k){
  const n = a.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  // dp[i][t][s] -> max sum using first i elements
  let dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> [NEG, NEG]));
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      for(let s=0;s<2;s++){
        const cur = dp[i][t][s];
        if(cur===NEG) continue;
        if(s===0){
          // skip a[i]
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
          // start new
          if(t<k) dp[i+1][t+1][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t+1][1], cur + a[i]);
        } else {
          // extend
          dp[i+1][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][1], cur + a[i]);
          // end
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++) ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n][t][0], dp[n][t][1]);
  return ans;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests time-expanded DP on grids for path counts with constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on (i,j,t) layers; space optimization; modulo arithmetic.
- Handling walls and boundary checks.

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[n-1][m-1] === 1) return 0;
  let prev = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(m).fill(0));
  prev[0][0] = 1;
  const dirs = [[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]];
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    const cur = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(m).fill(0));
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (const [dx,dy] of dirs) {
        const x = i + dx, y = j + dy;
        if (x>=0 && x<n && y>=0 && y<m && grid[x][y]===0) {
          cur[i][j] = (cur[i][j] + prev[x][y]) % MOD;
        }
      }
    }
    prev = cur;
  }
  return prev[n-1][m-1];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design with an extra edit dimension on a classic string problem; requires careful transitions for matches, edits, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings (i, j)
- Extra dimension for edits (t)
- Transitions for match/mismatch with edits
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxLPSWithEdits(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[i][i][t] = 1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++) {
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++) {
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
        let best = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t]);
        if (s[i] === s[j]) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t] + 2);
        if (t >= 1) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-1] + 2);
        if (t >= 2) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-2] + 2);
        dp[i][j][t] = best;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to require exactly k edits instead of at most?
- How to reduce space to O(n*k)?
- How to reconstruct the edited string and the subsequence?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a spacing constraint and path reconstruction, a common interview variation.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix with constrained subsequences
- 2D state and reconstruction pointers
- Handling infeasible states and space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// state definitions: dp[i][t], par[i][t]
function solve(A, n, k, d) {
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) dp[i][1] = A[i-1];
  for (let t=2; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      // skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; par[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i
      if (i - d >= 1 && dp[i-d][t-1] !== NEG) {
        const val = dp[i-d][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = i-d; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the indices from par to return the actual solution?
- How can you optimize space without changing asymptotic time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a classic DP over partitioning with a non-linear segment cost (max). It checks understanding of states, transitions, and reconstruction, plus edge cases like k=1 and k=n.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t] for first i elements into t segments
- Segment cost as max over A[j..i-1] and dynamic maintenance of that max
- Reconstruction of endpoints via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minSumMaxPartitions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let mx = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
        mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
        if (dp[j][t - 1] !== INF) {
          dp[i][t] = Math.min(dp[i][t], dp[j][t - 1] + mx);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact endpoints efficiently?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) with additional structure or pruning for typical inputs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with partitioning and a gap constraint, requiring prefix sums, 2D DP, and backtracking to output block endpoints. Encourages thinking about edge cases when n is large and k near n/gap.

## Key Concepts

- DP over prefixes with fixed block counts
- Enforcing gap constraints between blocks
- Backtracking to recover endpoints
- Time: O(nk); Space: O(nk)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKBlocks(profits, k, g) {
  const n = profits.length
  const S = Array(n + 1).fill(0)
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) S[i + 1] = S[i] + profits[i]
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity))
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1))
  dp[0][0] = 0
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i - 1][t])
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let l = 0; l <= i - 1 - g; l++) {
        const val = dp[l][t - 1] + (S[i] - S[l])
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = l }
      }
    }
  }
  const blocks = []
  let i = n, t = k
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = take[i][t]
    blocks.unshift([l, i - 1])
    i = l
    t--
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question introduces a non-linear block value that combines sum and minimum, forcing a DP that accounts for subarray metrics and partition points. It also tests reconstruction of exact k segments and handling of large intermediate data.

## Key Concepts

- Non-linear block cost: (sum) * (min)
- Precomputation of all subarray metrics V[l][r]
- DP over prefixes with exactly k blocks
- Path reconstruction from predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space (reducible to O(nk))

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const V = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = Infinity;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      const sum = pref[r+1]-pref[l];
      V[l][r] = sum * mn;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = NEG;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      pre[i][t] = pre[i-1][t];
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + V[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; pre[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = pre[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i-1]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) with a streaming recomputation of V? 
- How would the approach adapt if k is large and n up to 2e5 (any pruning or heuristic)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests partition DP with a subarray range cost, requiring precomputation of subarray extrema and careful reconstruction. It adds a different objective than prior questions by using (max-min) rather than a function of endpoints or a fixed multiplier. 

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact-k segments
- Subarray range queries (max-min) via precomputation
- Path reconstruction from parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs for small to mid-size n

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  const minv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    maxv[i][i] = A[i];
    minv[i][i] = A[i];
    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      maxv[i][j] = Math.max(maxv[i][j-1], A[j]);
      minv[i][j] = Math.min(minv[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = (maxv[0][i-1]-minv[0][i-1]) * i, par[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
        const cost = (maxv[p][i-1]-minv[p][i-1]) * (i-p);
        if (dp[p][t-1] + cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + cost;
          par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let idx = n, rem = k, cuts = [];
  while (rem > 0) {
    const p = par[idx][rem];
    cuts.push([p, idx-1]);
    idx = p; rem--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][k], blocks: cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explores a DP variant transforming flips into max-sum of k disjoint subarrays on a simple gain array.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two matrices: dp for best total, best for segments ending at i
- Transform 0/1 to +1/-1 gains to linearize gains
- Reconstructing segments via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOnesAfterKFlips(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const gain = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=> s[i]==='0'?1:-1);
  const NEG = -1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const best = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t] + gain[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + gain[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], best[i][t]);
    }
  }
  const ones = s.split('').filter(c=>c==='1').length;
  return ones + dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming over non-overlapping subarrays where block value depends on internal structure, requiring precomputation and reconstruction techniques.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping k blocks in increasing order
- Precomputation of per-block value (peaks inside)
- DP with inner maximization over l
- Reconstruction pointers; time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueSubarrays(A, k, F, P) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const peaks = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Precompute peaks
  for (let i = 1; i < n-1; i++) {
    if (A[i-1] < A[i] && A[i] > A[i+1]) {
      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        for (let r = i; r < n; r++) {
          peaks[l][r]++;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const val = (l, r) => pref[r+1] - pref[l] - F - P * peaks[l][r];

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (dp[l-1][t-1] + val(l-1, i-1) > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[l-1][t-1] + val(l-1, i-1);
          parent[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct
  const blocks = [];
  let cur = n, curT = k;
  while (curT > 0 && parent[cur][curT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[cur][curT];
    blocks.unshift([start, cur-1]);
    cur = start;
    curT--;
  }

  return { max: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Complexity

- Time: O(n²k) due to nested loops for peaks, DP, and reconstruction
- Space: O(nk) for DP table and parent pointers, reducible to O(n) with rolling arrays

